Walk a failed memo create or edit back whether or not its commit landed — the behaviour before
MEMNG-1190.

A commit that raised AFTER PostgreSQL committed (a dropped connection, a cancelled request) then
moves the original out from under a live memo and deletes the tier copies it serves. The probes
that must turn red are the `commit-landed` cases and the outcome-unknown cases, for both the create
and the edit (all must keep the files); `commit-did-not-land` stays green, because walking back a
write that was lost is the right answer and what that case pins.

--- a/app/routers/memos.py
+++ b/app/routers/memos.py
@@ -123,7 +123,7 @@
 
     With `xid` None this returns without ever suspending, so the walk-back behind it stays as
     uninterruptible as the inline calls it makes (MEMNG-145): no cancellation can land here."""
-    if xid is None:
+    if True:
         return False, None
     return await uploads_svc.commit_landed(xid, db)
 
